From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Mon, 04 Apr 94 12:03:53 GMT
Subject: [#94-4-31] introspection, certainty, complexity

Anton Sherwood writes:
>Steve Whitrow [94-4-17]:
>> . . . I'm certain that I couldn't possibly be mistaken over
>> my belief that I'm opposed to entropic death-worship . . .
>
>Certain?  Not just practically certain, no negligible epsilon
>of doubt?  I'm not nearly that sure of my convictions, as they
>have not been seriously tested (and to the extent that they 
>have been tested, my actions won't meet John Galt's standards of 
>purity), and my mind is too big for me to know all its contents.

For at least the past ten years, I believe I've regarded death as clearly
bad for the individual.  It could be that simulators have tampered with my
memory and I only began believing this five minutes ago, but at least I can
be certain that I believe it at the time of writing.  I couldn't believe
something and not believe it simultaneously.  However, the belief that
deathism is bad could be false -- we might have souls, or we might be
living in a simulation where the creators generally upgrade each
personality at the end of their life cycles.  So I estimate a probability
for falsehood of about 10^-20.  And if I had to choose between being
tortured for 1,000 years then given immense riches, or killed, I doubt if
I'd be brave enough to be tortured.

Once you start considering beliefs that aren't so clear-cut, like whether
human minds can satisfactorily run on computers, it becomes harder to be
certain as to whether the estimation of the belief's truth probability is
above or below 0.5.  There could be some vacillation, but at least I'm sure
my belief against deathism isn't fluctuating anywhere near the 0.5 mark.

So that seems to fit in -- I'm sure of some of my mind's contents, but not
all of it. 

