From: davisd@nimitz.ee.washington.edu
Date: Mon, 04 Apr 94 17:17:31 -0700
Subject: [#94-4-38] The Trouble With Left Anarchists  

> From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
> Subject: The Trouble With Left Anarchists
>
> the fruit of this initial act of aggression.  What I can't figure out is
> why they make the initial assumption of common ownership.  While I know that
> Dan Davis defends it on efficiency grounds, I don't think that position is
> tenable.

If you mean pragmatic grounds by your use of efficiency grounds, I
suppose you are correct, since ultimately I defend most things on
pragmatic grounds.

I have never heard a good argument defending private property in land.
In the absence of such an argument, property in land appears an
unreasonable imposition on the unlanded, and one with significant
costs to them.

Before I start people ranting, let me elaborate. I am not against
private property. There are good reasons for it, in the proper
context, but I find the context significantly different in the case of
natural resources.

I wish people to be able to control private parcels of land, and to
have their right of control protected. I want them to be able to buy
and sell land, including the improvements thereon. I just think there
should be some other reason besides the threat of force for the
unlanded to respect the property claims of the landed. I would think
that if all involved were reasonable, the landed would compensate the
unlanded for their right to control land, since they can demonstrate
no other reason besides force why the unlanded should agree to the
situation.

Of course, since I don't find people very reasonable, I doubt that
this will happen. And I have less and less emotional investment in the
position, since questions of how society should be aren't very
relevant to my life. I will work within the current system fully
confident that I will get mine. I think that I would be better off in
a system where the unlanded were compensated, but I might also be
better off if gravity pushed instead of pulled. I doubt that my
actions will change either situation. 

Discussion of this issue is kind of interesting, though. Once it is
agreed that something is property, there is little left to discuss.
Whether something should be treated as property, and precisely how,
reveals much more of interest about the discussion participants.

